ass: parse drawings and plain numbers faster

A vector clip traced around a sign is tens of thousands of points, 500 KB
of text, and motion tracking gives every frame its own. Parsing it was the
largest part of the frames that take longest (130 ms on a Fire TV Stick 4K
Max): a malloc and a free per point for a doubly linked token list, and
ass_strtod's general path, two passes and a loop over powers of ten, for
numbers like "1137.66".

- The tokens of a drawing live in one array, sized from the length of the
  text, and the outline is allocated for as many points. Same tokens, same
  order.
- ass_strtod takes a plain decimal of up to 15 significant digits in one
  pass and one division. The mantissa and the power of ten are both exact
  doubles, as they are in the general path for such a number, so the
  quotient is the same double; anything else (more digits, an exponent, no
  digits) falls through to the code as it was. The first nine digits are
  gathered in 32 bits, which a 32-bit ARM multiplies and converts without
  a library call. 300 million random strings give the same value, end
  pointer and errno with and without it.
- The two questions asked of an event's text before it is rendered (does
  it position itself, does it change over time) are answered by one walk
  over it, not one each.
- The rasterizer takes the bounding box of an outline as it adds the
  segments, not in a second pass over them.

